  <style>
    /*
     * Accent color injection from Ghost admin settings.
     * Only --color-primary is set here. All other semantic tokens
     * (--color-link, --color-link-hover, --color-secondary) are
     * derived from --color-primary inside tokens.css to keep one
     * source of truth and avoid cascade conflicts.
     */
    :root {
      /* Ghost accent color injection — commented out; tokens.css uses near-white instead
      {{#if @site.accent_color}}
        --color-primary: {{@site.accent_color}};
      {{/if}}
      */
      {{#if @site.icon}}
        --publication-icon: url('{{@site.icon}}');
      {{/if}}
      --color-particles: #1D4551; /* darkened further (~30%) from #296274 — still reading too bright */
    }

    :root[data-theme='light'] {
      /* Ghost accent color injection — commented out; tokens.css uses near-black instead
      {{#if @site.accent_color}}
        --color-primary: {{@site.accent_color}};
      {{else}}
        --color-primary: #60A26B;
      {{/if}}
      */
      /* Light mode reads on a ~L*98 backdrop, so contrast comes from how far
         BELOW the page the particle sits — the darker this is, the more each
         unit of sprite alpha buys. Measured against dark mode as the target
         (dark: mean deltaL of the hottest 1% = 22.4, share of pixels past a
         2-deltaL JND = 2.28%): #17516E left light mode at 15.9 / 0.80% —
         "barely visible". #082A3C plus the light glow-radius bump in
         particle-morph.hbs lands 24.2 / 2.29%. Colour alone cannot close it
         (see GLOW_RADIUS_LIGHT_MULT there) — the two are one tuning pair.
         KEEP IN SYNC with PARTICLE_NORMAL.light AND PARTICLE_SHIFTED.dark in
         scroll-scrub-anim.js, which both mirror this literal. */
      --color-particles: #082A3C; /* was #17516E, itself darkened from #2787B7 */
    }

    :root[data-theme='dim'] {
      /* Ghost accent color injection — commented out; tokens.css uses near-white instead
      {{#if @site.accent_color}}
        --color-primary: {{@site.accent_color}};
      {{/if}}
      */
    }
  </style>
  <script>
    (function() {
      if (window.location.pathname !== '/') return;
      // Only pre-hide the nav for a genuine fresh landing/refresh, not an
      // in-site click (e.g. the logo) — page-transition.js navigates via
      // window.location.href (a real page load, not a client-side route),
      // so document.referrer reliably reflects where we came from. Same
      // check preloader.js uses for its own skip path (assets/js/preloader.js) —
      // keep both in sync if this logic changes. Replaces the old
      // localStorage 'preloader_seen' once-per-session flag.
      //
      // isReload override (2026-08-06) — see preloader.js's own copy of
      // this comment for the full reasoning: document.referrer survives a
      // reload unchanged, so it can't distinguish "refresh" from "reached
      // this page via an internal link, at any point in this tab's past".
      // Navigation Timing's `type` can, and overrides it here.
      var isReload = false;
      try {
        var navEntry = performance.getEntriesByType && performance.getEntriesByType('navigation')[0];
        isReload = navEntry
          ? navEntry.type === 'reload'
          : !!(performance.navigation && performance.navigation.type === performance.navigation.TYPE_RELOAD);
      } catch (e) {}
      var cameFromSameSite = false;
      try {
        cameFromSameSite = !isReload && !!document.referrer && new URL(document.referrer).origin === window.location.origin;
      } catch (e) {}
      if (!cameFromSameSite) {
        document.documentElement.classList.add('preloading');
      }
    })();
  </script>
  {{ghost_head}}
  <script>
    // HOMEPAGE LANDING PRE-HIDE — restored: .home has an animated entrance
    // again (page-transition.js's runLandingAnimation, isHome branch —
    // fade .home + slide .hero, same values as About's <main> entrance),
    // so the hide-then-JS-reveal round trip matters again: without a CSS
    // pre-hide, the raw .home markup paints fully visible for one frame
    // before DOMContentLoaded-timed JS ever runs — read as a blink. Scoped
    // to same-site arrival only, same as window.__pageEntranceOwns below —
    // a fresh landing gets the full preloader choreography instead.
    (function () {
      try {
        var isHome = (location.pathname === '/' || location.pathname === '');
        // Same isReload override as the preloading-class script above (and
        // preloader.js) — a reload must always fall through to the full
        // preloader treatment below, never the quick fade+slide entrance
        // this branch drives, regardless of what document.referrer still
        // carries from an earlier internal click in this tab's history.
        var isReloadForLanding = false;
        try {
          var navEntry2 = performance.getEntriesByType && performance.getEntriesByType('navigation')[0];
          isReloadForLanding = navEntry2
            ? navEntry2.type === 'reload'
            : !!(performance.navigation && performance.navigation.type === performance.navigation.TYPE_RELOAD);
        } catch (e2) {}
        if (isHome && !isReloadForLanding && document.referrer && new URL(document.referrer).origin === location.origin) {
          document.documentElement.classList.add('landing-pending');
          // Failsafe — same 2.5s pattern as html.main-pending below, so a
          // stalled/never-firing runLandingAnimation() can't leave the
          // homepage permanently blank.
          setTimeout(function () { document.documentElement.classList.remove('landing-pending'); }, 2500);
          // scroll-scrub-anim.js's initHero() also needs to know this ISN'T
          // a fresh landing, so it can snap the hero particle shape (helix
          // etc.) straight to its resting state instead of replaying the
          // multi-second form-in morph a first visit gets. Read there, never
          // written back.
          window.__pageEntranceOwns = true;
        }
        if (!isHome) {
          // NON-HOME PRE-HIDE — same reasoning, for work/about/contact/post
          // pages: page-transition.js's runLandingAnimation() hides <main>
          // via gsap.set(main,{y:200,opacity:0}), but that's JS-timed and
          // runs on DOMContentLoaded — the raw HTML (including the first
          // grid-card) would otherwise paint fully visible for one frame
          // before that JS executes. Unlike the homepage branch this isn't
          // gated on referrer — runLandingAnimation()'s non-home branch
          // runs on every load of these pages regardless of arrival path,
          // so this must too, or direct/external loads would still flash.
          // Removed the instant runLandingAnimation() takes over (same
          // tick as its own gsap.set, so no gap), with the same failsafe
          // timeout so a page type this doesn't apply to (tag archives,
          // etc.) never stays hidden.
          document.documentElement.classList.add('main-pending');
          setTimeout(function () { document.documentElement.classList.remove('main-pending'); }, 2500);
        }
      } catch (e) {}
    })();
  </script>
  <script>
    // NAV PREV/NEXT HOP — set by page-transition.js's click handler right
    // before navigating via a post-navigation.hbs prev/next link. Must run
    // pre-paint (before <body> even exists) since it decides whether the
    // collapsed post-page nav pill (main.css "POST PAGE NAV") renders
    // ALREADY collapsed (this hop) or starts expanded and animates into
    // its collapsed state (navigation.hbs's own script, triggered by the
    // ABSENCE of this class — see main.css: html.nav-hop is the instant/
    // no-animation path, body.nav-collapsing is the animated one).
    (function () {
      try {
        if (sessionStorage.getItem('navPrevNextHop') === '1') {
          document.documentElement.classList.add('nav-hop');
        }
      } catch (e) {}
    })();
  </script>
  <script>
    // TOOLTIP LANDING GUARD
    //
    // Nav tooltips are pure CSS :hover (main.css — `.nav-menu a:hover
    // .nav-tooltip`, `.nav-icon-btn:hover .nav-tooltip`). Every in-site nav
    // click is a REAL browser navigation (page-transition.js sets
    // window.location.href), so the pointer is still physically over the
    // same icon when the new page paints — :hover matches instantly and the
    // tooltip re-appears with no mouse movement at all. From the visitor's
    // side that reads as one tooltip shown twice: hover → see it → click →
    // it vanishes with the old page → it comes back on the new one.
    //
    // Suppress tooltips until the pointer actually MOVES. Set before <body>
    // exists (same reason as main-pending/nav-hop/curtain-restoring above)
    // so the tooltip can never paint even for one frame; the first real
    // mousemove clears it and normal hover behaviour resumes for the rest
    // of the page's life.
    //
    // Deliberately unconditional — not gated on a same-site referrer. A
    // fresh load with the cursor parked over the nav has exactly the same
    // no-movement-yet problem, and the guard costs nothing when the cursor
    // is elsewhere (the class is dropped on the visitor's first move).
    (function () {
      var d = document.documentElement;
      d.classList.add('tooltips-suppressed');
      function release() {
        d.classList.remove('tooltips-suppressed');
        window.removeEventListener('mousemove', release);
        window.removeEventListener('pointermove', release);
        window.removeEventListener('touchstart', release);
        window.removeEventListener('keydown', release);
      }
      // pointermove covers mouse+pen; mousemove is the fallback for older
      // engines. touchstart/keydown release it too so a touch or keyboard
      // user is never left with tooltips permanently disabled (they'd
      // otherwise never fire a move event at all).
      window.addEventListener('pointermove', release, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('mousemove', release, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('touchstart', release, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('keydown', release);
    })();
  </script>
  <script>
    // CURTAIN-RETURN GUARD (moved here from a <body> script further down,
    // which ran well after the page's own main content — the hero/.home
    // markup — had already been parsed and painted. Adding
    // html.curtain-restoring that late didn't stop the browser from
    // showing the hero first; this must run before <body> exists, same as
    // main-pending/nav-hop above, to actually prevent that first paint
    // rather than race it.
    //
    // When page-transition.js is about to restore a mid-page scroll
    // position (post → close → back to source), the page boots fresh and
    // both the hero and the particle entrance would otherwise flash/appear
    // before (or after) the scrollThrough() sweep settles the real state.
    // Pre-hide both from the very first frame; the sweep's own triggers
    // (hero-exit / footer / …, __particleApply further down in <body>)
    // then take ownership on their first call and drop the veil. Keys are
    // only READ here — consuming them is page-transition.js's job.
    (function () {
      try {
        var __crOrigin = JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem('postOrigin') || 'null');
        if (sessionStorage.getItem('curtainReturn') === '1' &&
            __crOrigin && typeof __crOrigin.scrollY === 'number' && __crOrigin.scrollY > 100) {
          window.__particleLayerHidden = true;
          document.documentElement.classList.add('curtain-restoring');
          // failsafe: never let the veil outlive the restore window
          setTimeout(function () { document.documentElement.classList.remove('curtain-restoring'); }, 8000);
        }
      } catch (e) {}
    })();
  </script>
</head>
<body class="{{body_class}}" data-page-width="{{@custom.page_width}}" data-card-style="{{@custom.card_style}}" data-typography-header="{{@custom.typography_style_header}}" data-typography-body="{{@custom.typography_style_body}}" data-type-size="{{@custom.type_size}}" data-type-size-headings="{{@custom.type_size_headings}}" data-contact-email="{{#if @site.email}}{{@site.email}}{{else}}przemek.luczak@gmail.com{{/if}}">
  <!-- SVG refraction filter for the .glass-liquid module (assets/css/
       glass-liquid.css) — dormant until <html> also carries the
       glass-distort class (not added anywhere yet; opt in per-page/
       site when ready to test the distorted variant). Zero visual/
       paint cost while unused: a filter def with no element
       referencing it renders nothing. position:absolute + 0×0 keeps
       it out of layout without display:none, which some browsers
       treat as "don't render referenced filters either." -->
  <svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" style="position:absolute; width:0; height:0; overflow:hidden;">
    <defs>
      <filter id="glass-distortion" x="0%" y="0%" width="100%" height="100%" filterUnits="objectBoundingBox">
        <feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.004 0.009" numOctaves="1" seed="17" result="turbulence"></feTurbulence>
        <feComponentTransfer in="turbulence" result="mapped">
          <feFuncR type="gamma" amplitude="1" exponent="10" offset="0.5"></feFuncR>
          <feFuncG type="gamma" amplitude="0" exponent="1" offset="0"></feFuncG>
          <feFuncB type="gamma" amplitude="0" exponent="1" offset="0.5"></feFuncB>
        </feComponentTransfer>
        <feGaussianBlur in="turbulence" stdDeviation="3" result="softMap"></feGaussianBlur>
        <feSpecularLighting in="softMap" surfaceScale="5" specularConstant="1" specularExponent="100" lighting-color="white" result="specLight">
          <fePointLight x="-200" y="-200" z="300"></fePointLight>
        </feSpecularLighting>
        <feComposite in="specLight" operator="in" in2="SourceGraphic" result="specCut"></feComposite>
        <feDisplacementMap in="SourceGraphic" in2="softMap" scale="120" xChannelSelector="R" yChannelSelector="G" result="displaced"></feDisplacementMap>
        <feComposite in="displaced" in2="specCut" operator="over"></feComposite>
      </filter>
    </defs>
  </svg>

  <!-- Fixed viewport backdrop — REQUIRED, do not remove (it went missing once
       and silently broke two things at once). It (1) paints the page-scoped
       palette shift: .page-backdrop in main.css blends var(--color-background)
       toward var(--shift-bg) by --profile-shift — body is transparent ON
       PURPOSE so this layer shows (see body rule in main.css); and (2) gives
       the particle canvas's dark-mode mix-blend-mode:screen a backdrop that
       can't rubber-band away during macOS overscroll (raw opaque-black canvas
       showed without it). -->
  <div id="page-backdrop" class="page-backdrop"></div>

  <!-- Fixed background gradient layer (fades between colors as you scroll)
 <div id="page-gradient" class="page-gradient-bg"></div>-->

  <!-- Soft gradient "aura" background — separate component from
       #page-gradient above (gradient-layer.js's per-card crossfade,
       scoped to .posts-tabs-section — untouched by this). Single element,
       multiple radial-gradient layers stacked in one background-image
       (softness comes from each gradient's own "transparent 50%" falloff,
       no filter:blur() at all), animated by stepping background-position
       through @keyframes — see main.css ".page-gradient-blobs" for the
       implementation. Second attempt at this component: the first
       (blurred, absolutely-positioned divs animated via transform) was
       compositor-only and cheaper per frame; this one repaints the
       gradient each frame instead, the direct trade for the richer,
       more blended multi-color look this technique gives.
       Colors: same data attribute as before, comma-separated, 3 hex
       colors — 6-digit (opaque) or 8-digit #RRGGBBAA (built-in alpha)
       both work, set directly as each radial-gradient's color stop.
       Falls back to the trio below if ever emptied.
       Invisible by default (main.css) — present on every page, but inert
       until something calls window.pageGradientBlobs.show()/.hide()/
       .setColors([...]), the tiny controller this script exposes. First
       (and so far only) caller: assets/js/gradient-blobs-trigger.js,
       loaded below — same "load site-wide, no-op if its target element
       isn't on this page" pattern gradient-layer.js already uses, so a
       future trigger for a different section is just another small
       script, not a change here. 
  <div id="page-gradient-blobs" class="page-gradient-blobs" aria-hidden="true"
       data-blob-colors="#D16BA5,#86A8E7,#5FFBF1"></div>-->
  <script>
    (function () {
      var el = document.getElementById('page-gradient-blobs');
      if (!el) return;

      function setColors(colors) {
        ['--aura-1', '--aura-2', '--aura-3'].forEach(function (prop, i) {
          if (colors[i]) el.style.setProperty(prop, colors[i]);
        });
      }

      // Initial paint from the data attribute (fallback trio or a
      // per-page override set directly on the element above).
      setColors((el.getAttribute('data-blob-colors') || '')
        .split(',').map(function (c) { return c.trim(); }).filter(Boolean));

      window.pageGradientBlobs = {
        show: function () { el.classList.add('is-visible'); },
        hide: function () { el.classList.remove('is-visible'); },
        setColors: setColors,
      };
    })();
  </script>

